          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lagerlof-gosta-berlings-saga-d15ddf5744cb0103\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"The Countess directly avows as true that she loved Gösta at an earlier, impermissible time. The retrospective confession explains why his public praise of her purity causes shame. It does not undertake or renew a relational commitment, and the surrounding sobbing and repetition do not independently make the words an expressive/reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The Countess directly addresses Gösta; the love avowal is syntactically embedded in the rhetorical question “vet du inte, att jag älskade dig” (“do you not know that I loved you”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Gösta has publicly portrayed his wife as good and innocent. She objects because she had loved him before she believed she was permitted to do so, making that praise feel exposing and undeserved. Her confession is distressed and shame-laden, but its core force remains a retrospective avowal offered to explain her reaction, not a new promise or commitment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Gösta, Gösta, vet du inte, att jag älskade dig, när jag ännu inte hade lov därtill?”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person past-tense clause explicitly presents her earlier love for Gösta as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Men hur kunde du säga till folket, att jag var god och ren?” followed by “Jag blygdes för folket, Gösta.”\",\"supports\":\"Her avowal explains why Gösta’s public characterization of her as good and pure caused shame; this supports an explanatory confession rather than an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Och hon skakades av snyftningar.”\",\"supports\":\"The delivery is intensely emotional, but the text does not show the words being involuntarily blurted or functioning independently as affective discharge; therefore it does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as Swedish “jag älskade dig” in Selma Lagerlöf’s Gösta Berlings saga, with the form marked present_or_past.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified core is the explicit past-tense love expression in the supplied source occurrence.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core retrospective avowal. The shame, moral framing, marital context, and emotional delivery are adequately represented as contextual features and do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
